WEBVTT

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:04.555
Frameworks such as MITRE ATT&CK help hunters reason about adversary behavior in a

00:00:04.555 --> 00:00:05.465
structured way.

00:00:05.465 --> 00:00:09.085
ATT&CK organizes tactics, techniques, and procedures.

00:00:09.085 --> 00:00:13.200
Tactics describe the adversary objective, such as persistence, defense evasion,

00:00:13.200 --> 00:00:15.955
credential access, discovery, or lateral movement.

00:00:15.955 --> 00:00:20.000
Techniques describe how that objective may be attempted. Procedures describe specific

00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:21.950
observed ways a technique was carried out.

00:00:21.950 --> 00:00:25.640
Hunters use this structure to turn threat knowledge into defensive questions.

00:00:25.640 --> 00:00:29.185
A key hunting principle is to look for behavior, not only known indicators.

00:00:29.185 --> 00:00:34.320
An indicator of compromise, or IOC, is often a specific value: a file hash, domain,

00:00:34.320 --> 00:00:37.640
IP address, email sender, registry path, or command string.

00:00:37.640 --> 00:00:40.500
IOCs can be useful, especially for scoping known activity,

00:00:40.500 --> 00:00:44.320
but they can change quickly. Behavioral evidence focuses on what the activity does.

00:00:44.320 --> 00:00:49.360
For example, encoded command execution, unusual persistence locations, discovery commands,

00:00:49.360 --> 00:00:50.650
credential access signals,

00:00:50.650 --> 00:00:54.890
and lateral movement patterns may be visible even when the exact hash or domain is new.

00:00:54.890 --> 00:00:59.115
Behavioral hunting does not require offensive instructions. The defender asks what

00:00:59.115 --> 00:01:02.000
telemetry would show if a category of activity occurred.

00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:04.300
If the question is about encoded command execution,

00:01:04.300 --> 00:01:08.110
the hunter may look for process events where scripting engines launch with suspicious

00:01:08.110 --> 00:01:09.170
command-line patterns.

00:01:09.170 --> 00:01:11.445
If the question is about unusual persistence,

00:01:11.445 --> 00:01:15.440
the hunter may look for new startup entries or scheduled tasks outside known software

00:01:15.440 --> 00:01:16.270
deployment tools.

00:01:16.270 --> 00:01:18.250
If the question is about discovery behavior,

00:01:18.250 --> 00:01:22.800
the hunter may look for endpoints issuing many system or directory enumeration commands.

00:01:22.800 --> 00:01:26.958
If the question is about lateral movement, the hunter may look for unusual remote logons,

00:01:26.958 --> 00:01:29.470
administrative shares, or service creation events.

00:01:29.470 --> 00:01:32.315
Mapping behavior to telemetry prevents shallow searches.

00:01:32.315 --> 00:01:34.810
Instead of searching for one suspicious string everywhere,

00:01:34.810 --> 00:01:38.410
the hunter asks which data source should contain the behavior, which fields matter,

00:01:38.410 --> 00:01:40.190
and which baseline would make it unusual.

00:01:40.190 --> 00:01:44.840
Endpoint telemetry may show process relationships. Authentication logs may show account

00:01:44.840 --> 00:01:48.010
movement. DNS and proxy logs may show external communication.

00:01:48.010 --> 00:01:51.730
Cloud control-plane logs may show administrative behavior. The mapping links the

00:01:51.730 --> 00:01:54.480
hypothesis to evidence. There is still room for IOCs.

00:01:54.480 --> 00:01:57.450
If a credible report names a domain, hash, or certificate,

00:01:57.450 --> 00:01:59.990
a hunter can search for that value to scope exposure.

00:01:59.990 --> 00:02:04.200
The important distinction is that IOC matching often answers whether a known artifact

00:02:04.200 --> 00:02:04.680
appeared,

00:02:04.680 --> 00:02:09.030
while behavior hunting asks whether a class of suspicious activity may be present.

00:02:09.030 --> 00:02:11.610
Mature hunts often combine both. For beginners,

00:02:11.610 --> 00:02:16.125
ATT&CK is most useful as a thinking aid. Do not treat it as a checklist to run

00:02:16.125 --> 00:02:16.850
mechanically.

00:02:16.850 --> 00:02:21.280
Use it to frame defensible questions, identify likely telemetry, document assumptions,

00:02:21.280 --> 00:02:24.650
and produce detection ideas that survive beyond one temporary indicator.

00:02:24.650 --> 00:02:27.685
Behavior mapping also helps explain findings to other teams.

00:02:27.685 --> 00:02:29.565
Instead of saying a query looked strange,

00:02:29.565 --> 00:02:32.419
the hunter can say the activity aligns with a discovery pattern,

00:02:32.419 --> 00:02:35.230
a credential access signal, or a lateral movement concern,

00:02:35.230 --> 00:02:36.950
and then show the supporting telemetry.

00:02:36.950 --> 00:02:40.320
That language connects evidence to risk without overstating certainty.
